WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.080
Why did Dumbledore groom Harry to die? How&nbsp;
could they trust student safety to a werewolf?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:11.680
And is Slytherin's hatred and distrust of the&nbsp;
other houses justified? Keep watching to find out!

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.760
Former Death Eater or not, seeking out a job&nbsp;
working with children to procure the power you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:23.600
never had as a kid isn't a cute or defensible&nbsp;
move. Fans tend to give Severus Snape a pass on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.320
his questionable behavior as a teacher because&nbsp;
he turns on Voldemort to become a double agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:33.280
for Dumbledore. Yet his motives to betray&nbsp;
Voldemort have little to do with nobility.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:38.960
Snape was angry that Voldemort killed Lily, but&nbsp;
he was all too happy to let James and Harry die&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:43.520
to snag the one that got away for himself&nbsp;
— that's not love. And just because he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.640
saves Harry's life a few times doesn't excuse&nbsp;
bullying a child because he looks like his dad.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.440
And it's not just Harry that Snape lords over&nbsp;
like a classroom bully who's still reliving his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.240
glory days. Neville's boggart proves that&nbsp;
he's more afraid of Snape than the woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.960
who tortured his parents into insanity&nbsp;
— which is horrifying, to say the least.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:07.440
Despite the fandom's romanticization&nbsp;
of Snape, his few heroic moments and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.080
his obsession over a woman who told him "no"&nbsp;
don't erase his cruelty toward his students.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.600
You'd be hard-pressed to find a "Harry&nbsp;
Potter" fan who doesn't love Rubeus Hagrid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:22.000
but that doesn't mean that he's without his&nbsp;
faults. Hagrid's love for magical creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.960
may be legendary, but he doesn't always put&nbsp;
their needs ahead of his own selfishness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.000
Should Hagrid be keeping a dragon as a pet or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.160
chaining up a three-headed dog in a tiny room&nbsp;
to protect a treasure? Absolutely not. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:41.760
Hagrid's love for creatures endlessly puts their&nbsp;
lives at risk — and the lives of his students.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:46.080
Whether he's risking the life of Aragog,&nbsp;
the arachnid he saved, by smuggling him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:51.200
in the castle when he's a Hogwarts student, or&nbsp;
bringing a hippogriff to his unruly classroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.920
quite a few animals would have been better off&nbsp;
not knowing him. Buckbeak nearly dies because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.920
of Hagrid's negligence when Buckbeak&nbsp;
was just doing what's in its nature.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:03.760
Between that and drinking copious&nbsp;
amounts of alcohol around students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.640
our favorite half-giant isn't so cuddly after all.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:12.640
By Dumbledore's estimations, Sybill Trelawney&nbsp;
has made precisely two real predictions during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.040
her tenure at Hogwarts: The prophecy&nbsp;
regarding Voldemort's vanquisher and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.120
the "Prisoner of Azkaban" prophecy where&nbsp;
she predicts the return of Peter Pettigrew.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.760
"He who betrayed his friends at&nbsp;
Hogwarts with murder shall break free!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.640
Innocent blood shall be spilt!"

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.960
Given that Trelawney doesn't even&nbsp;
remember her actual predictions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:37.520
she's either an intentional fraud who likes&nbsp;
to toy with her students for dramatic effect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:41.040
or she's convinced herself to&nbsp;
believe her own mind games.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.440
Either way, she treats her students&nbsp;
like pawns in a metaphysical experiment.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:51.120
And she also emotionally abuses Hermione just&nbsp;
because she's a bit of a know-it-all. Calling a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.640
student's soul as dry as a book is pretty gross.&nbsp;
If your student questions the legitimacy of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:03.840
subject, you don't lash out and eviscerate their&nbsp;
self-confidence — they're children. Be an adult.

00:03:05.163 --> 00:03:07.280
Sure, Remus Lupin [REE-muss LOO-pin] is&nbsp;
hands-down the school's most competent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.760
Defense Against the Dark Arts teacher that we&nbsp;
see throughout the series, but why is he left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.880
to howl at the moon on Hogwarts grounds at all?&nbsp;
Having the guy hide in a tree during full moons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:22.720
when he turns into a werewolf in a castle full&nbsp;
of students is questionable judgment at best.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.680
Lupin's wolfy characteristics&nbsp;
certainly aren't his fault,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.480
but for a couple of days out of the month, he&nbsp;
shouldn't be anywhere near students — especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.920
not when the ever-vindictive Snape is in charge of&nbsp;
his symptom-relieving potion. During transitions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.720
he should be in a safe, secluded space to&nbsp;
protect himself and everyone around him.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.840
Instead, both he and Dumbledore&nbsp;
leave things to fate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.320
and Harry and his friends almost die for&nbsp;
it. Lupin's situation is quite tragic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:53.280
given how little control he has over his own&nbsp;
body, but that doesn't excuse him from bearing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:58.480
responsibility of ensuring his students' safety&nbsp;
before he turns each month and loses all will.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:04.640
The bar is pretty low for Hogwarts professors,&nbsp;
but even educators like Slughorn leave a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:09.200
to be desired. Sure, Slughorn is competent&nbsp;
when you measure him against the dude who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:15.360
wears Voldemort as a fashionable headpiece, but&nbsp;
on his own, Slughorn's a pretty awful teacher.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:18.960
Slughorn uses his students to feed&nbsp;
his narcissism, taking credit for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:23.520
their accomplishments and displaying them in his&nbsp;
classroom like trophies that he personally won.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:27.200
He even creates a creepy elite&nbsp;
club for his star students.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:32.640
Okay, we get it, Hogwarts' terrible safety track&nbsp;
record makes open positions difficult to fill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:36.480
but you can't just hire terrible people&nbsp;
because you can't fill a position.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.560
Even Slughorn himself doesn't want to&nbsp;
take the job. Someone really needs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:44.720
assist Dumbledore on the hiring process&nbsp;
because his track record is pretty bad.

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:50.080
Albus Dumbledore made an alarming number of&nbsp;
mistakes and oversights during his time at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:55.440
Hogwarts. Still, none are quite as sinister as&nbsp;
what he does to the wizarding world's golden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:00.800
boy Harry Potter. In his calculated quest&nbsp;
to defeat Voldemort, Dumbledore raises Harry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:04.800
to be a martyr for the slaughter, while&nbsp;
never being completely honest with him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.840
putting Harry in grave danger annually for&nbsp;
reasons the boy never fully understands.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:14.720
Harry risks his life time and time again to fix&nbsp;
the mistakes of the negligent Hogwarts staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:18.160
yet even when Dumbledore takes&nbsp;
him on perilous Horcrux missions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:22.800
Dumbledore still keeps the young wizard in&nbsp;
the dark. Probably because his master plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:27.440
requires Harry to die at the right time&nbsp;
in order to destroy the last Horcrux.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:32.640
It's frankly a shock that Harry even lives long&nbsp;
enough to carry out that insane plan, but the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:37.840
that Dumbledore secretly maneuvers Harry into&nbsp;
sacrificing himself is also pretty shocking.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:42.800
Despite the fact that Voldemort cursed the&nbsp;
Defense Against the Dark Arts position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:48.560
Dumbledore still does little to no vetting when he&nbsp;
fills the open position. Instead, he continually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:53.680
hires incompetent or straight-up evil wizards&nbsp;
to teach the school's most important subject.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:59.280
As it stands, we've got professors dying,&nbsp;
some acting as Voldemort's right-hand face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:05.200
and a narcissist who obliviates iconic wizards&nbsp;
to steal their life story — just to name a few.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:08.960
It's wild that Dumbledore is supposed&nbsp;
to be the greatest wizard in his time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:13.360
yet he can't be bothered to lift a curse&nbsp;
or come up with a clever way around it.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:18.000
Hell, Cedric Diggory would still be alive&nbsp;
if Dumbledore instilled some anti-polyjuice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:23.200
potion measures to prevent identity theft in a&nbsp;
magic school. Use your big brain, Dumbledore!

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:28.080
Apparently, the Forbidden Forest&nbsp;
is only forbidden when professors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:32.640
aren't hosting detention there. In "The&nbsp;
Sorcerer's Stone," Harry, Ron, and Hermione&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:37.680
get caught out of bed when Malfoy snitches on&nbsp;
them. Genius that he is, he gets lumped into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:43.120
the detention crew himself by tattling. Instead&nbsp;
of doing something normal like writing lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:47.840
the four students get separated into groups to&nbsp;
hunt down a unicorn killer in a cursed forest.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:50.560
What the heck are these teachers thinking?!

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:54.160
The forest isn't the only esoteric&nbsp;
punishment at the castle, though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:58.480
Filch reminisces about the good old days when&nbsp;
he got to hang students up in the dungeons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:03.040
And there's also Dolores Umbridge, who&nbsp;
enjoys making students write lines that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:07.920
get carved into their skin with a sick&nbsp;
quill. A charming slate of staff, huh?

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:13.920
With the level of House favoritism Hogwarts&nbsp;
professors exhibit when they award or detract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:19.440
points for the House Cup, the tradition is all&nbsp;
but rigged. A few professors like McGonagall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:23.600
seem to keep things fair, but even she&nbsp;
shows her Gryffindor cards on occasion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:29.440
Snape is less than subtle about his determination&nbsp;
to detract every possible point from Gryffindor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:34.080
while awarding Slytherin points whenever&nbsp;
he can — and he's not the only one.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:35.840
"Are you incapable of restraining yourself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:39.360
or do you take pride in being&nbsp;
an insufferable know-it-all?"

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:41.440
"He's got a point, you know?"

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:42.880
"Five points from Gryffindor."

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:48.640
However, Dumbledore gets the award for the&nbsp;
worst case of points tampering we've ever seen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:52.160
It's bad enough when professors dole&nbsp;
out unfair points throughout the year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:57.680
but Dumbledore needs to make a whole dramatic show&nbsp;
of things. In "Sorcerer's Stone," Gryffindors are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:03.760
dead last until Dumbledore baits and switches the&nbsp;
celebrating Slytherins by publicly awarding Harry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:07.920
a bunch of points for straight-up murdering&nbsp;
Quirrell, Ron for playing a great game of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:13.440
overdramatic chess, Hermione for being a cool&nbsp;
witch, and Neville for standing up to his friends.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.640
Of course, he does this after announcing Slytherin&nbsp;
as the winner. No wonder Slytherins are always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:23.200
so pissy - the headmaster of the school&nbsp;
literally pits every house against them!

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:26.480
"Gryffindor wins the House Cup!"

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:32.560
In the Hogwarts professors' quest to put their&nbsp;
students in as much peril as wizardly possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:37.840
they put on a dangerous and occasionally deadly&nbsp;
international tournament between teenaged martyrs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:42.320
Despite the original Triwizard Tournament's&nbsp;
demise due to the death of teenagers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:45.200
the wizard schools decide that&nbsp;
this is a swell time to bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:49.200
the tradition back in "Goblet of Fire."&nbsp;
It might as well be "The Hunger Games!"

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:54.560
Based on what we know, it doesn't seem like the&nbsp;
professors or headmasters do a single thing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:59.920
make it safer this go-around — and what do you&nbsp;
know? Cedric Diggory pays the price with his life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:04.240
Sure, having three international schools&nbsp;
compete in a tournament to learn about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:09.840
different cultures and gain new experiences is&nbsp;
a great idea. However, there doesn't need to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:15.600
a death component, with dragons and other&nbsp;
dangerously unpredictable tasks to do so.

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:19.680
The tournament also leads to Dumbledore's&nbsp;
most out-of-character moment in the film

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:22.160
"Harry, Did you put your&nbsp;
name in the Goblet of Fire?!"

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:24.160
"No sir!"
 
"Did you ask one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:25.280
older students to do it for you?"
 &nbsp;

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:27.680
"No sir!"
 
"You're absolutely sure?"

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:32.480
Despite his outrage in the movie, in&nbsp;
the books Dumbledore already knows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:36.960
that Harry isn't advanced enough to fool&nbsp;
the Goblet's age barrier. Suffice to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:41.760
it's not one of Dumbledore's best moments from the&nbsp;
films, and the tournament is just as deadly as it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:45.600
was in years past because the professors&nbsp;
couldn't keep a tighter watch on things.

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:49.280
With a headmaster as experienced as Dumbledore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:53.040
you'd think that he'd be more than willing to&nbsp;
take the lead on any investigative work when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:57.920
all is not well at Hogwarts. Yet no matter&nbsp;
how many times Harry, Ron, and Hermione&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:02.560
bring serious situations to the school’s&nbsp;
attention, they brush it off. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:08.000
the educators always leave a bunch of kids to do&nbsp;
the dirty work of Hogwarts' negligent professors.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:12.080
The Hogwarts teachers openly let their students&nbsp;
fight their battles while rarely lifting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:17.760
finger to handle situations themselves. Why?&nbsp;
Because of their ego trip, of course. If they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:22.400
haven't noticed anything themselves, how&nbsp;
on earth could something possibly be funky?

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:26.800
One time would be more than enough, but even&nbsp;
Hogwarts' best professors like McGonagall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:32.240
gaslight students about any serious concerns&nbsp;
without ever doing anything substantial. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:36.320
it comes to the final battle, the students are&nbsp;
somehow still taking charge when many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:40.720
remaining professors don't do all that much&nbsp;
to help out. Just look at the death toll of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:45.840
students compared to professors. There are far&nbsp;
too many crises at the school to begin with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:50.160
but why is there rarely a professor around&nbsp;
when shady wizard drama is going down?

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:56.480
The professors continually say that Hogwarts is&nbsp;
the safest place in the wizarding world — but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:00.160
that assumption is precisely what causes&nbsp;
them to frequently put students' lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:04.640
in danger by being complacent. Hiding&nbsp;
a three-headed dog and the Sorcerer's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:09.440
Stone in the castle was a major lack of&nbsp;
judgment - and it was just one of many.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:14.080
Sirius Black manages to infiltrate the&nbsp;
school, Peter Pettigrew runs around as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:19.520
the Weasleys' rat for years, and Death Eaters&nbsp;
can stroll in whenever they want. If an old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:24.320
map that a bunch of mischievous kids created&nbsp;
can detect someone who's supposed to be dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:27.600
why aren't more measures like&nbsp;
that taken by actual professors?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:32.320
For an impenetrable school, a whole lot&nbsp;
of devious wizards manage to infiltrate.

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:33.840
How is this a safe school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:38.560
and why aren't the professors and its headmaster&nbsp;
taking these breaches in security seriously?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:43.840
How many students have to die before professors&nbsp;
stop toting the "there's no safer place" tagline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:48.960
and actually pay attention to what's happening&nbsp;
around them? If Harry Potter — the world's most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:54.640
oblivious wizard — notices a problem when the&nbsp;
teachers don't, that's definitely a red flag.

00:11:54.640 --> 00:12:00.000
Even the repetition of tragic historical events&nbsp;
goes unbothered by the staff. At the first sign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.520
of the Chamber's reopening, the school should be&nbsp;
evacuated — let alone after multiple petrified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:10.800
students are discovered. One wrong glance, and the&nbsp;
school would have a dozen more Moaning Myrtles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:15.200
It's a miracle it takes so long before&nbsp;
parents start pulling their kids from school.

